---
title: 'Scrollbar'
enterprise: true
---

The scrollbar provides an interface for panning around a chart.

The Scrollbar becomes available when the chart is zoomed in or when fixed-width bars require more space than the series
area. For fixed-width bar configuration, see the [Series Bars](./bars/#fixed-width) documentation.

## Enabling the Scrollbar

The Scrollbar is disabled by default. To enable it, add the following configuration:

```js format="snippet"
{
    scrollbar: {
        enabled: true,
    },
}
```

{% chartExampleRunner title="Scrollbar" name="scrollbar" type="generated" /%}

## Visibility

The `scrollbar.visible` property controls when the Scrollbar is displayed if enabled:

-   `'auto'` (default) - shows the Scrollbar only when the data window is smaller than the full range.
-   `'always'` - always shows the Scrollbar.
-   `'never'` - hides the Scrollbar even when the data window is smaller than the full range.

With `visible: 'auto'`, the Scrollbar appears in these common scenarios:

-   Fixed-width bars push the data beyond the available series area, so not all categories fit in view.
-   An initial zoom is set via `initialState`.
-   Zooming is enabled and the user zooms in.

{% chartExampleRunner title="Scrollbar Visibility" name="scrollbar-visibility" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    scrollbar: {
        enabled: true,
        visible: 'always',
    },
}
```

## Initial Zoom

{% chartExampleRunner title="Scrollbar Initial Zoom" name="scrollbar-initial-zoom" type="generated" /%}

To start in a zoomed state with the scrollbar visible, set an initial zoom state with the [Chart Initial State API](./api-state/#initial-state). This works without
enabling zoom interactions:

```js format="snippet"
{
    initialState: {
        zoom: {
            ratioX: { start: 0.15, end: 0.5 },
        },
    },
}
```

## Track Interactivity

The Scrollbar track mirrors native scrollbar behaviour:

-   Clicking the track pages the thumb by one viewport width towards the click position.
-   If the click lands on the thumb, nothing moves.
-   Page increments clamp to the click position, so the thumb never overshoots the cursor.
-   Holding the mouse button down repeats the page increment after 400ms, then every 50ms.
-   Repeats stop when the thumb reaches the cursor, on mouse up, or when the pointer leaves the track perpendicularly.
-   Shift+Click jumps the thumb so the click is centred within the thumb (clamped at the ends).
-   All calculations use logical ratios (percentage of range), not pixels, for consistent behaviour at any size.
-   Dragging continues if the pointer moves away from the track, with the thumb clamped to the valid range.
-   On touch devices, consider increasing `scrollbar.thickness` to provide a more comfortable hit
    target for dragging.

## Vertical and Horizontal Scrollbars

By default, scrollbars appear in any direction that is scrollable based on the current data window.

Only one Scrollbar can be shown in each direction.

Configure horizontal and vertical scrollbars through the
`scrollbar.horizontal` and `scrollbar.vertical` properties.

Any options supplied on the top-level `scrollbar` object
apply to both orientations unless they are overridden within the specific `horizontal` or `vertical` configuration.

{% chartExampleRunner title="Vertical Scrollbar" name="vertical-scrollbar" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    scrollbar: {
        enabled: true,
        vertical: {
            enabled: true,
            position: 'left',
        },
    },
}
```

The `position` can be set to `'left'` or `'right'` for vertical scrollbars, and `'top'` or `'bottom'` for horizontal
scrollbars.

## Placement and Spacing

The `placement` property determines whether the Scrollbar appears inside or outside the axis ticks and labels:

-   `'outer'` (default) - positions the scrollbar outside the ticks and labels, but inside the axis title.
-   `'inner'` - positions the scrollbar inside the ticks and labels, but still outside the chart series area.

{% chartExampleRunner title="Scrollbar Placement" name="scrollbar-placement" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    scrollbar: {
        enabled: true,
        placement: 'inner',
        spacing: 8,
        tickSpacing: 6,
    },
}
```

The `spacing` value controls the gap between the scrollbar and the series area or axis labels (depending on placement),
while `tickSpacing` controls the gap between axis ticks and an inner Scrollbar.

## Customisation

The Scrollbar's appearance can be customised through its `track` and `thumb` properties.

{% chartExampleRunner title="Scrollbar Styling" name="scrollbar-styling" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    scrollbar: {
        enabled: true,
        placement: 'inner',
        thickness: 8,
        spacing: 8,
        track: {
            fillOpacity: 0,
            strokeWidth: 0,
        },
        thumb: {
            fill: '#6b7280',
            strokeWidth: 0,
            cornerRadius: 8,
            hoverStyle: {
                fill: '#374151',
            },
        },
    },
}
```

In this configuration:

-   `thickness` sets the height of horizontal scrollbars or width of vertical scrollbars.
-   `track` styles the background area of the Scrollbar.
-   `thumb` styles the draggable indicator showing the current position.
-   `thumb.hoverStyle` customises the thumb colour on hover.

Both `track` and `thumb` support standard fill and stroke options including `fill`, `stroke`, `fillOpacity`,
`strokeWidth`, `lineDash`, `lineDashOffset`, `opacity`, and `cornerRadius`.

## Thumb Size and Disabled State

The thumb has a minimum size to remain clickable (configurable via `scrollbar.thumb.minSize`). When the
data window spans the full range, the Scrollbar disables its interactions; if `visible: 'always'` is set, the thumb
fills the track to indicate that panning is not available.

